#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138164 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138387 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61a5c363-0371-5820-a4e4-b21335406e50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Bounds When Rounding to a Whole Number"}] :question/id #uuid "b18fef84-bbbb-58c6-a215-4146461c28f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-14084" :question/problem "The height $h$ of a lamppost is $30\\,\\textrm{ft}$ to the nearest $1\\,\\textrm{ft}.$ Determine the upper and lower bounds of this measurement." :question/worked-solution "Our measurement is given to the nearest $1\\,\\textrm{ft}.$ So, the greatest possible error of this measurement is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1\\,\\textrm{ft}}{2} = 0.5\\,\\textrm{ft}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the lower and upper bounds of this measurement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{lower bound} & =30\\,\\text{ft}-0.5\\,\\text{ft} \\\\  & =29.5\\,\\text{ft} \\\\ \\text{upper bound} & =30\\,\\text{ft}+0.5\\,\\text{ft} \\\\  & =30.5\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the exact value of $h$ lies between the lower and upper bound, we conclude that\n\n$$\n29.5\\,\\textrm{ft} \\leq h <    30.5\\,\\textrm{ft}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138165 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138388 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e99cabd4-b713-50e1-83be-f8571b575803" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Bounds When Rounding to a Decimal Place"}] :question/id #uuid "9e0f0485-0635-558e-8493-eef61ee623f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-14085" :question/problem "The area $a$ of a golf course measures $0.6\\,\\textrm{km}^2$ to the nearest tenth of a square kilometer. Determine the upper and lower bounds of this measurement." :question/worked-solution "Our measurement is given to the nearest $0.1\\,\\textrm{km}^2.$ So, the greatest possible error of this measurement is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.1\\,\\textrm{km}^2}{2} = 0.05\\,\\textrm{km}^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the lower and upper bounds of this measurement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{lower bound} & =0.6\\,{\\text{km}}^{2}-0.05\\,{\\text{km}}^{2} \\\\  & =0.55\\,{\\text{km}}^{2} \\\\ \\text{upper bound} & =0.6\\,{\\text{km}}^{2}+0.05\\,{\\text{km}}^{2} \\\\  & =0.65\\,{\\text{km}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the exact value of $a$ lies between the lower and upper bound, we conclude that\n\n$$\n0.55\\,\\textrm{km}^2 \\leq a <            0.65\\,\\textrm{km}^2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138167 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138389 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29217571-4597-5bbe-aecd-0d9a1d1aa1c1" :knowledge-point/title "Bounding Areas and Volumes"}] :question/id #uuid "44ff13ac-1980-554e-aea8-4aaa51d202fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-14087" :question/problem "The dimensions of a parking lot are $90\\, \\textrm{m} \\times 50\\, \\textrm{m}$, where each dimension is measured to the nearest $10\\, \\textrm{m}.$ Rounded to the nearest ten square meters, what is the upper bound for the area of the parking lot?" :question/worked-solution "Each dimension is given to the nearest $10\\,\\textrm{m}.$ So, the greatest possible error of these measurements is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10\\,\\textrm{m}}{2} = 5\\,\\textrm{m}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the *upper bound* for the area of the parking lot. Therefore, we require the *upper bound* of each dimension.\n\nThe upper bounds for each dimension are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 90\\,\\text{m}+5\\,\\text{m}=95\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & 50\\,\\text{m}+5\\,\\text{m}=55\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the upper bound for the area of the parking lot is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =95\\,\\text{m}\\times 55\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =5\\,225 \\\\  & ≈5\\,230\\,{\\text{m}}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest ten square meters."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138387 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61a5c363-0371-5820-a4e4-b21335406e50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Bounds When Rounding to a Whole Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "512e9253-a4a7-5c0b-97ea-83527ff1f3fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35\\,\\text{m}\\le d<45\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476373} {:answer/id #uuid "56138ea0-d350-5323-832f-ed4b6da5287d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,\\text{m}\\le d<50\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476385} {:answer/id #uuid "90513a05-a76e-52b5-9628-feaba6b47d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35.5\\,\\text{m}\\le d<44.5\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476511} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e4f648-784b-5a43-87ac-77da3ef5f518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39\\,\\text{m}\\le d<41\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476684} {:answer/id #uuid "f038b6fc-e31f-5379-89d5-d85f9d5e491a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39.5\\,\\text{m}\\le d<40.5\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "512e9253-a4a7-5c0b-97ea-83527ff1f3fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35\\,\\text{m}\\le d<45\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476373} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5a10c85-4b92-5a7f-9aa4-97e39df01cc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "697f5959-f087-5527-b15d-214bd9021ebd" :question/math-academy-id "q-154064" :question/problem "The distance $d$ between two lampposts is $40\\,\\text{m}$ to the nearest $10\\,\\text{m}.$ Determine the upper and lower bounds of this measurement." :question/worked-solution "Our measurement is given to the nearest $10\\,\\text{m}.$ So, the greatest possible error of this measurement is\n\n$$\n\\frac{10\\,\\text{m}}{2}=5\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the lower and upper bounds of this measurement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{lower bound} & =40\\,\\text{m}-5\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =35\\,\\text{m} \\\\ \\text{upper bound} & =40\\,\\text{m}+5\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =45\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the exact value of $d$ lies between the lower and upper bound, we conclude that\n\n$$\n35\\,\\text{m}\\le d<45\\,\\text{m}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138387 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61a5c363-0371-5820-a4e4-b21335406e50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Bounds When Rounding to a Whole Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50ae9cc8-39d1-55e5-be52-6173a02aa0cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{cm}\\le l<15\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476371} {:answer/id #uuid "71967f4e-bf57-5679-af0f-b50e405893c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.5\\,\\text{cm}\\le l<14.5\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476460} {:answer/id #uuid "7370fc6e-00e4-5423-8124-b0631c2aabd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.95\\,\\text{cm}\\le l<14.05\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476465} {:answer/id #uuid "74bf6f74-26df-55de-8361-fc78879d0972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.9\\,\\text{cm}\\le l<14.1\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476466} {:answer/id #uuid "b26726e6-ef31-50f9-aa7f-af28f0df4788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{cm}\\le l<24\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71967f4e-bf57-5679-af0f-b50e405893c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.5\\,\\text{cm}\\le l<14.5\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476460} :answer-field/id #uuid "f878e921-2acc-50fd-8e43-f4ed513a9b8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b85f6c30-099c-5add-8b59-401be051e650" :question/math-academy-id "q-154066" :question/problem "The length $l$ of a knife blade is $14\\,\\text{cm}$ to the nearest $1\\,\\text{cm}.$ Determine the upper and lower bounds of this measurement." :question/worked-solution "Our measurement is given to the nearest $1\\,\\text{cm}.$ So, the greatest possible error of this measurement is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1\\,\\text{cm}}{2}=0.5\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the lower and upper bounds of this measurement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{lower bound} & =14\\,\\text{cm}-0.5\\,\\text{cm} \\\\  & =13.5\\,\\text{cm} \\\\ \\text{upper bound} & =14\\,\\text{cm}+0.5\\,\\text{cm} \\\\  & =14.5\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the exact value of $l$ lies between the lower and upper bound, we conclude that\n\n$$\n13.5\\,\\text{cm}\\le l<14.5\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138388 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e99cabd4-b713-50e1-83be-f8571b575803" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Bounds When Rounding to a Decimal Place"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "197aacc1-a89c-56b5-bbfc-363af67376d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "197aacc1-a89c-56b5-bbfc-363af67376d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476248} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a84bda0-ecde-520d-a425-82bf05a6c34f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476770} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52390098-dc96-539b-af82-ac72adda1cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52390098-dc96-539b-af82-ac72adda1cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476377} :answer-field/id #uuid "e734ae0c-9ed5-5bce-b1fc-753baadcafc6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "813619c9-d532-5fa8-aa46-e2d238a68f09" :question/math-academy-id "q-218706" :question/problem "The time $t$ it took Noah to run $200\\,\\text{m}$ was $31.2\\,\\text{s}$ to the nearest tenth of a second. Determine the upper and lower bounds of this measurement.\n\n{{field-1}}$\\text{s}$\n$\\,\\le t<$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{s}$" :question/worked-solution "Our measurement is given to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{s}.$ So, the greatest possible error of this measurement is\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.1\\,\\text{s}}{2}=0.05\\,\\text{s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the lower and upper bounds of this measurement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{lower bound} & =31.2\\,\\text{s}-0.05\\,\\text{s} \\\\  & =31.15\\,\\text{s} \\\\ \\text{upper bound} & =31.2\\,\\text{s}+0.05\\,\\text{s} \\\\  & =31.25\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the exact value of $t$ lies between the lower and upper bound, we conclude that\n\n$$\n31.15\\,\\text{s}\\le t<31.25\\,\\text{s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476925 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138388 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e99cabd4-b713-50e1-83be-f8571b575803" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Bounds When Rounding to a Decimal Place"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d28e809-0be7-5de5-b7b6-efed5550afd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2693.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d28e809-0be7-5de5-b7b6-efed5550afd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2693.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476361} :answer-field/id #uuid "72266152-ce5d-5351-b6cb-62c0f80e6040" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476807} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c54b4dd-d42b-596e-9a9b-87b3dcaa4810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2693.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c54b4dd-d42b-596e-9a9b-87b3dcaa4810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2693.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476444} :answer-field/id #uuid "a37eb16d-22ea-52e4-acfe-081207b7a264" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9595515f-fef9-57d0-bdc3-6db4a7b77611" :question/math-academy-id "q-218713" :question/problem "The distance $d$ between the easternmost and westernmost points of the contiguous United States measures $2,693.1\\,\\text{mi}$ to the nearest tenth of a mile. Determine the upper and lower bounds of this measurement.\n\n{{field-1}}$\\text{mi}$\n$\\,\\le d<$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{mi}$" :question/worked-solution "Our measurement is given to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{mi}.$ So, the greatest possible error of this measurement is\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.1\\,\\text{mi}}{2}=0.05\\,\\text{mi}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the lower and upper bounds of this measurement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{lower bound} & =2,693.1\\,\\text{mi}-0.05\\,\\text{mi} \\\\  & =2,693.05\\,\\text{mi} \\\\ \\text{upper bound} & =2,693.1\\,\\text{mi}+0.05\\,\\text{mi} \\\\  & =2,693.15\\,\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the exact value of $d$ lies between the lower and upper bound, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2,693.05\\,\\text{mi}\\le d<2,693.15\\,\\text{mi}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138389 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29217571-4597-5bbe-aecd-0d9a1d1aa1c1" :knowledge-point/title "Bounding Areas and Volumes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "450ed54a-6b67-51e3-97f3-9938e38a763d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "450ed54a-6b67-51e3-97f3-9938e38a763d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476351} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c5f8348-62fb-52f4-8bc5-1b6a23724307" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f23b7a04-be79-58bf-9d3e-f31e5bea28da" :question/math-academy-id "q-218721" :question/problem "The dimensions of a stamp are $2.1\\,\\text{cm}\\times 2.4\\,\\text{cm}$, where each dimension is measured to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{cm}.$ Rounded to the nearest tenth of a square centimeter, what is the upper bound for the area of the stamp?\n\nTo the nearest tenth of a square centimeter, the upper bound is\n{{field-1}}${\\text{cm}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Each dimension is given to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{cm}.$ So, the greatest possible error of these measurements is\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.1\\,\\text{cm}}{2}=0.05\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the *upper bound* for the area of the stamp. Therefore, we require the *upper bound* of each dimension.\n\nThe upper bounds for each dimension are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2.1\\,\\text{cm}+0.05\\,\\text{cm}=2.15\\,\\text{cm} \\\\  & 2.4\\,\\text{cm}+0.05\\,\\text{cm}=2.45\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the upper bound for the area of the stamp is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2.15\\,\\text{cm}\\times 2.45\\,\\text{cm} \\\\  & =5.267\\,5\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2} \\\\  & ≈5.3\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth of a square centimeter."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138389 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29217571-4597-5bbe-aecd-0d9a1d1aa1c1" :knowledge-point/title "Bounding Areas and Volumes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18868649-9176-55f0-bcee-dc4acd091801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "371\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476244} {:answer/id #uuid "1acae981-193e-5375-b7d3-e5c7dc725228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "368\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476254} {:answer/id #uuid "4d77b9e5-d60d-55dd-ba48-ad825b552253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "364\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476362} {:answer/id #uuid "b96546a2-8483-5d95-a192-078c54ead42b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "383\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476624} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4ebd3d-80cd-55b5-bd9b-475e7e2224d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "375\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18868649-9176-55f0-bcee-dc4acd091801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "371\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476244} :answer-field/id #uuid "d98b6691-4a9c-574e-8a86-2da84cb14cd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2d8e097-556a-5209-9bb4-f4ab8f864a87" :question/math-academy-id "q-46382" :question/problem "The dimensions of the cover of a chemistry book are $28\\,\\text{cm}\\times 14\\,\\text{cm}$, where each dimension is measured to the nearest $1\\,\\text{cm}.$ Rounded to the nearest square centimeter, what is the smallest possible area of the book's cover?" :question/worked-solution "Each dimension is given to the nearest $1\\,\\text{cm}.$ So, the greatest possible error of these measurements is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1\\,\\text{cm}}{2}=0.5\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the *smallest* possible area of the book's cover. Therefore, we require the *lower bound* of each dimension.\n\nThe lower bounds for each dimension are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 28\\,\\text{cm}-0.5\\,\\text{cm}=27.5\\,\\text{cm} \\\\  & 14\\,\\text{cm}-0.5\\,\\text{cm}=13.5\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,  the smallest possible area of the book's cover is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =27.5\\,\\text{cm}\\times 13.5\\,\\text{cm} \\\\  & =371.25\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2} \\\\  & ≈371\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest square centimeter."}]}
